  "titulo": "The secretary who died with $7.2 million",
  "descripcion": "Grace Groner bought three shares for $180 in 1935, reinvested every dividend and never sold. When she died at 100, it was worth $7.2 million, and nobody knew. The honest part: that's a trade almost nobody can make.",
  "tags": ["grace groner", "dividend investing", "compound interest", "buy and hold", "personal finance"],
  "hashtags": ["#shorts", "#investing", "#dividends", "#personalfinance"]
 },
 "voz": {"motor": "kokoro", "voz": "am_adam"},
 "guion": [
  {"id": "intro", "texto": "She was a secretary. She died at a hundred with seven million, two hundred thousand dollars."},
  {"id": "thesis", "texto": "No second job. No inheritance. No stock tip nobody else had. Just one holding, bought for a hundred and eighty dollars, and seventy-five years of not touching it."},
  {"id": "proof", "texto": "She worked as a secretary for forty-three years. Bought her clothes at rummage sales. Every dividend the company paid her, she put back into more shares. Nobody at that company knew what she was sitting on."},
  {"id": "payoff", "texto": "Three shares became thousands. Seventy-five years of doing nothing but reinvest, and this is where it ends up."},
  {"id": "honesty", "texto": "Here's the uncomfortable part. She could have sold and lived better, any year she wanted. She chose not to, for seventy-five years straight. That's not a strategy — that's a trade almost nobody can actually make."},
  {"id": "lesson", "texto": "You don't need her seventy-five years or her one stock. The engine is the same at any size. Own it, reinvest it, and leave it alone."},
  {"id": "cta", "texto": "Could you sit on a hundred and eighty dollars for seventy-five years and never touch it? Or would you have sold at ten thousand?"}
 ],
